From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hermes <hermes@local>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 10:24:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] host: the certificate entry carries the DER address, not its
 offset

The store landed but the game died one call later: sig=11 at address 0x30, with 849 (the
size) alive in three registers and 0x30 - the value just written as der_offset - in the
register that got dereferenced. That is the shape of the mistake: der_offset is the
service wire format (nn::ssl::sf::BuiltInCertificateInfo, what Ryujinx and Citron build),
while the class the game receives from nn::ssl::BuiltInManager is BuiltInCertificateInfoBase,
whose fourth word is a pointer to the DER. Translating one into the other is this entry's job,
since this entry is the library's.

So the builder takes the shape as a parameter: the address of the DER for SDK 17's class, the
offset for the service's struct. The SDK 17 spelling (hle_offline.c) uses the pointer.

The other spelling this branch answers (nn::ssl::BuiltInCertificateInfo, a class derived from
that base) goes back to the terminator alone. We have measured the base, not the derived class:
if it carries one field more, the caller's stride is wider than 0x18 and an entry written here
would hand it DER bytes to read as the next entry. The terminator cannot be overshot by any
stride, and it is exactly the empty answer that spelling always gave - no certificate, nothing
to walk into, the same err 0x270141 the title showed before instead of a crash.

t_ssl_certs is header-only now - the store, both shapes of the fourth word, the terminator,
an unknown id, the file that wins, a buffer too small - so it runs anywhere without a network
or the TLS framework.

Upstream-Status: fork-only
Reason: The store answered with one certificate, and Super Mario Party Jamboree died on sig=11 at address 0x30 -
 the value just written as der_offset - while the size (849) sat alive in three registers. der_offset is the
 service's wire format; the class the title receives from nn::ssl::BuiltInManager is BuiltInCertificateInfoBase,
 whose fourth word is a pointer to the DER, and turning one into the other is the library entry's job. The
 builder now takes the shape: the address of the DER for SDK 17's class, the offset for the service's struct.
 The other spelling (nn::ssl::BuiltInCertificateInfo, derived from that base) goes back to the terminator alone,
 because we have measured the base and not the derived class: a wider stride would send the caller into the DER
 bytes. t_ssl_certs is header-only and pins both shapes.
Issue: https://github.com/GigiFrack/NX2Apple (fork)

